-- title:
--   Nonzero scalars act surjectively on the corner H(1-e)
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring that is a domain, a principal ideal ring and a local ring, with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m =$ `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R`, let $H$ be a commutative $R$-algebra, and let $e \in H$ be idempotent, $e \cdot e = e$. Write $L =$ `Localization.Away (1 - e)` for the localisation of $H$ at the multiplicative set of powers of $1 - e$, regarded as an $R$-algebra through $R \to H \to L$. Assume that the ideal of $L$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak m$ under $R \to L$ is the unit ideal, i.e. $\mathfrak m L = L$. The conclusion is that for every $d \in R$ with $d \neq 0$ and every $y \in H$ there exists $z \in H$ such that $d \cdot (z(1-e)) = y(1-e)$, the scalar action being that of $R$ on $H$. Thus multiplication by any nonzero element of $R$ is surjective on the corner $H(1-e)$.
--
--   An elementary commutative-algebra input: over a local principal ideal domain the only primes are $0$ and $\mathfrak m$, so the hypothesis that $\mathfrak m$ generates the unit ideal after inverting $1 - e$ forces every nonzero $d \in R$ to become invertible there, whence divisibility by $d$ on the idempotent corner. It is used in the finite-part reduction for Hopf algebra kernels, by [`HopfAlgebra.map_hopfKer_eq_hopfKer_of_finitePartIdempotent`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.map_hopfKer_eq_hopfKer_of_finitePartIdempotent).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsLocalRing_exists_smul_mul_one_sub_eq_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq_top.lean

